          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Do European and US bodies differ on when to start medication for stage 1 hypertension?",
          "answer": "European and US guidelines differ significantly in their definition of stage 1 hypertension, which in turn affects when medication is considered.\n\n**Diagnostic Thresholds:**\n*   **United States guidance** defines hypertension as beginning at 130/80 mm Hg. (Source: `guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets`)\n*   **European guidance** describes office hypertension as starting at 140/90 mm Hg. It considers blood pressure readings between 130-139 / 80-89 mm Hg as \"high-normal blood pressure\" rather than stage 1 disease. (Source: `guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets`)\n\n**Implications for Medication Initiation:**\nDue to these differing diagnostic thresholds, the approach to initiating medication for stage 1 hypertension also varies:\n*   **United States guidance** would classify blood pressure at or above 130/80 mm Hg as stage 1 hypertension. For individuals with stage 1 hypertension who also have established cardiovascular disease, diabetes, chronic kidney disease, or a 10-year cardiovascular risk of 10 percent or more, drug treatment is recommended alongside lifestyle measures. For those without these additional risk factors, lifestyle measures are advised first, with reassessment after three to six months before considering drug treatment. (Source: `guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment`)\n*   **European guidance** treats much of the population that US guidance calls stage 1 (i.e., those with blood pressure between 130/80 and 139/89 mm Hg) as having \"high-normal\" blood pressure, for whom lifestyle advice alone is often recommended initially. For those meeting the European definition of stage 1 hypertension (starting at 140/90 mm Hg), similar considerations apply: drug treatment is initiated with lifestyle measures if there is established cardiovascular disease, diabetes, chronic kidney disease, or a high cardiovascular risk. Otherwise, lifestyle measures are first, with drug treatment considered after three to six months. (Source: `guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment`)\n\nIn summary, the key difference is that a blood pressure reading between 130/80 and 139/89 mm Hg is classified as stage 1 hypertension in the US, leading to earlier consideration of medication for some, whereas European guidelines consider this \"high-normal\" and generally advise lifestyle changes alone.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
